    <item>
      <title>Acupoint Thread Embedding for Weight Loss in Women With Overweight or Obesity : A Randomized Controlled Trial</title>
      <link>https://proxy1.library.jhu.edu/login?qurl=https%3A%2F%2Fdoi.org%2F10.7326%2FANNALS-26-02294</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">pmid:43038532</guid>
      <pubDate>Mon, 16 Nov 2026 17:03:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <category>Annals of internal medicine</category>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Background:&lt;/b&gt; Whether acupoint thread embedding (ATE), a traditional Chinese medicine intervention, produces weight reduction remains uncertain.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Objective:&lt;/b&gt; To evaluate the efficacy and safety of ATE for weight reduction in women with overweight or obesity.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Design:&lt;/b&gt; Randomized, sham-controlled trial masked to participants, outcome assessors, and analysts. (ClinicalTrials.gov: NCT06316310).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Setting:&lt;/b&gt; Acupuncture Department, Shanghai Municipal Hospital of Traditional Chinese Medicine, Shanghai, China.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Participants:&lt;/b&gt; 132 women aged 18 to 45 years with a body mass index (BMI) of 24.0 kg/m2 or greater.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Intervention:&lt;/b&gt; Participants were randomly assigned 1:1 to ATE (6 sessions over 12 weeks) or sham ATE (superficial needle insertion at the same acupoints without manipulation, de qi, thread placement, or needle retention), followed by a 12-week follow-up.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Measurements:&lt;/b&gt; The primary outcome was change in BMI from baseline to week 12, analyzed under the intention-to-treat principle using linear mixed-effects models.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Results:&lt;/b&gt; At week 12, BMI and body weight decreased 0.7 kg/m2 (95% CI, -1.0 to -0.3 kg/m2) and 1.8 kg (CI, -2.7 to -0.9 kg), respectively, more with ATE than with sham ATE; at week 24, differences were -1.1 kg/m2 (CI, -1.7 to -0.6 kg/m2) and -3.0 kg (CI, -4.5 to -1.6 kg), respectively. Weight reduction of 5% or more occurred in 25.8% versus 9.1% (risk difference, 16.7 percentage points [CI, 3.7 to 29.3 percentage points]) at 12 weeks. Acupoint thread embedding produced greater reductions in waist and hip circumference, body fat percentage, and appetite scores, whereas cardiometabolic and patient-reported outcomes did not differ. Adverse events were mild and comparably distributed.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Limitation:&lt;/b&gt; Single center, generally healthy women of reproductive age, unmasked acupuncturists, short-term follow-up, and underpowered for uncommon adverse events and subgroup analyses.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Conclusion:&lt;/b&gt; Acupoint thread embedding produced modest but sustained reductions in BMI and adiposity-related measures in Chinese women of reproductive age with overweight or obesity.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Primary funding source:&lt;/b&gt; National Facility for Translational Medicine (Shanghai).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Xu S, Yang G, Hu C, Li X, Yin X, Zhao Y&lt;br/&gt;Journal Article&lt;br/&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://proxy1.library.jhu.edu/login?qurl=https%3A%2F%2Fdoi.org%2F10.7326%2FANNALS-26-02294&quot;&gt;Full text&lt;/a&gt; · &lt;a href=&quot;https://proxy1.library.jhu.edu/login?qurl=https%3A%2F%2Fpubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov%2F43038532%2F&quot;&gt;PubMed&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
